<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_circ-challenge" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Challenge - Electric Circuits</title>
	
	<!--
	If you want an A*R*C*S (Analyze-Represent-Calculate-Sensemake) activity, use the <project> tag.
	If you want an Explain activity, use the <investigation> tag.
		Use this text for qualitative comparisons: <em>greater than</em>, <em>less than</em>, or <em>equal to</em>
	If you want a Calculate activity, use the <exploration> tag.
	
	<m># \mathrm{~}</m>
	<m> \mathrm{~}</m>
	-->


	<investigation>
		<title>Circuit I</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<figure xml:id="fig-circuit_3">
				<caption>Circuit diagram.</caption>
				<image source="circ/circuit-3.png" width = "40%" >
				<description>A circuit diagram with three batteries in series on the left.  The first vertical branch contains bulbs A and B in series.  The next vertical branch contains bulb C in series with a parallel network of bulb D and bulbs E and F in series.</description></image>
			</figure>
			<p>
				Rank the light bulbs shown above according to brightness. If any bulb does not light, or if you do not have enough information, state so explicitly. 
			</p>
			<p>
				In particular, explain your reasoning for how you ranked each of the following pairs of bulbs:
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						A &amp; B
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						A &amp; C
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						A &amp; D
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		
		<assemblage>
			<p>
				<alert><!--REPLACE_TAG-->Tip:</alert> Use voltage diagrams to help explain your reasoning.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</statement>
	</investigation>

		<investigation>
		<title>Circuit II</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<figure xml:id="fig-circuit_7">
			<caption>Circuit diagram.</caption>
			<image source="circ/circuit-7.png" width = "40%" >
			<description>Above: A circuit diagram with three vertical branches, from left to right: three batteries, bulbs A and B in series, and bulb C in series with a parallel network of bulb D and bulbs E and F in series.  Below: the same diagram where bulb A has been replaced by another battery, oriented positive up.</description></image>
		</figure>
			<p>
				Bulb A in the top circuit is replaced by a battery, as shown in the bottom circuit. Does the brightness of light bulbs B and C increase, decrease, or remain the same? Please include separate claims and reasoning for each bulb.
			</p>
			<assemblage>
			<p>
				<alert><!--REPLACE_TAG-->Tip:</alert> Use voltage diagrams to help explain your reasoning.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
		</statement>	
	</investigation>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Metacognitive Reflection</title>
		<activity>
		<title>Light Bulb Model</title>
		
		
		<statement><p></p>
			<p>
				You built a model for circuits this week, based on the brightness of light bulbs.  Restate the model here in your own words, and explain why you cannot use Ohm's law for real lightbulbs.
			</p>
		</statement>
		</activity>
	</subsubsection>
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>